artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $f$ be a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism of the cylinder $\mathbb{T}^1 \times [-1,1]$ (where $\mathbb{T}^1 = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$) satisfying the following properties:

(i) $f$ preserves the (normalized) Lebesgue measure on the cylinder;
(ii) $f$ is homotopic to the identity;
(iii) $f$ has only finitely many periodic points;
(iv) $f$ leaves each boundary circle invariant, and the rotation number $\rho(f|_{\mathbb{T}^1 \times \{-1\}}) = \alpha$ satisfies a Diophantine condition, i.e. there exist constants $C>0$ and $\tau \ge 0$ such that $|\alpha - p/q| \ge C/q^{2+\tau}$ for every $p \in \mathbb{Z}$ and $q \in \mathbb{N}$.

Must $f$ be $C^\infty$-conjugate to the rigid rotation $R_\alpha:\mathbb{T}^1\times[-1,1] \to \mathbb{T}^1\times[-1,1]$ defined by $R_\alpha(\theta,r) = (\theta + \alpha, r)$?

